Consider three enterprises:
- **TitanMine**, a private company with 35 employees that extracts titanium ore in one region and sells it only in its own country;
- **AeroNational**, a state-owned manufacturer of military transport aircraft with 9,000 employees, plants in three countries and customers in twenty;
- **Wingtip Consulting**, a one-person business that advises flying schools on safety management in several markets of one country.

A classmate claims: "Firms are unnecessary; each engineer could simply sell their work to whoever needs it on the market."

The deck names forward and backward vertical integration [Slide 27]; in the standard reading (extension), backward integration moves toward the inputs and forward integration toward the customer. Buying the engine supplier is therefore **backward vertical integration**, and buying the satellite-integration company would be **forward vertical integration**. Both change the **vertical scope** of the firm, replacing a market interface with an administrative one [Slide 19].

The advantages Helios seeks with the engine supplier [Slide 27]:
- **control of inputs**: no more unexpected price rises or delays;
- **reduction of transaction costs**: the disputes over design changes become internal decisions;
- **independence** from a single supplier and a **better competitive position**;
- **reduction of information asymmetries**: Helios will know the real costs and status of the engines.

Engineers bring to management:
- effective project planning, time management and problem solving;
- attention to detail;
- experience in data processing;
- computer skills and technical knowledge.

The internal organization of an enterprise is **teamwork**, and team production requires a **controller**. The incentive to take on that task comes from four rights:
- to receive the **residual income** or surplus;
- to **control and reassign** the other factors of production;
- to be the **central party in all the contracts** signed with the inputs (raw materials, capital, human resources);
- to **sell** these rights.

The **entrepreneur is the person who holds all these rights**.

In large corporations the **Board of Directors** guides the business, and the company is run by **managers**: professional employees who take decisions and assume risks. This separates ownership (the shareholders) from control (the managers) and creates a problem [Slide 25]:
- managers may look for their **own benefit**, and the shareholders do not control the way they act;
- managers may take decisions in their own interest, disregarding the effect on shareholders;
- shareholders and managers have **different access to information** and **different objectives**;
- shareholders have neither enough information to assess the managers' performance nor incentives to do so, or to improve the management of the business.

The result is a **conflict of interest between ownership and control**: the **agency problem**.

The solution is for shareholders to be able to control the way managers act, so that they do not pursue their own benefit [Slide 26]. The **mechanisms of control** are:
- selecting the **Board of Directors**;
- **contracts** signed with managers, linked to goals;
- requesting **audits**;
- as an additional mechanism, **indicators**.

Increasingly, the shareholders who own large blocks of shares are the ones taking the decisions.

Traditionally, the objective of the business is identified with the objective of the entrepreneur, and the Classical Theory states that this objective is **profit maximization**. The view has four limitations:
- profit is difficult to define and to quantify;
- it ignores the **risk** associated with achieving the profits;
- the very concept of **maximization** is questionable;
- it gives an excessive leading role to the entrepreneur, whereas in practice **ownership and control are separated**.

A diversification decision involves two questions: **how attractive is the sector** to be entered, and **can the firm achieve a competitive advantage** in it? Firms diversify for three reasons:
- **growth**: to escape from declining industries (e.g. tobacco, oil, newspapers);
- **risk**: diversification reduces the volatility of earnings;
- **profit**: bringing different businesses together may increase profitability.
